    "readme_text": "# Biomechanical origins of proprioceptor feature selectivity and topographic maps in the Drosophila leg: Physiology data\n---\n\nRaw data for in vivo two-photon calcium imaging and cell tracking shown in the manuscript:Biomechanical origins of proprioceptor feature selectivity and topographic maps in the Drosophila leg (Neuron 2023). \n\nIncludes data for:\n* Calcium imaging from each type of femoral chordotonal cells (Fig. 1).\n* Claw and cap cell movements (Fig.4 and S4).\n* Claw cell activity and positition at different tibia angle (Fig. 5 and S6).\n* Club cell vibration response (Fig. 6 and S6)\n\n## Description of the data and file structure\n\n* FeCO_cell_type_activity_Fig1: contains .csv files that show the activity of each type of FeCO cell (DF/F value recorded from the axon) during tibia flexion/extension.\nEach column in ...DFF_trace.csv file represents DF/F values from one fly. Each column in ...tibia_angle.csv represents tibia angle for each fly's recording.\n\n* claw_and_cap_cell_movements_Fig4_S4: contains .csv files that show the x and y position of the claw and cap cells vs the tibia angle for each fly. We mean-centered the cell position within each fly by subtracting the average claw\ncell position when tibia is at 90° for each fly.\n\n* claw_activity_position_tibia_angle_Fig5_S6: contains 3 folders, each corresponding to different groups of claw cells (claw extension, claw flexion, mixed population of claw cells). Each folder contains python pickle files that shows the position and activity of different claw cells at different tibia angles.\nAlso contain a jupyter notebook with codes to load and plot the data.\n\n\n * club_vibration_response_Fig6_S6: contains .csv files that show the center of the activity of club neurons during tibia vibration at different frequencies. \n\n\n## Sharing/Access information\n\n* Analysis code used in this study is available at github.com/Tuthill-lab.\n\n*Any additional information required to reanalyze the data is available from the lead contact of the above mentioned paper, John Tuthill (tuthill@uw.edu), upon request.\n\n",
